WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
A plant can't walk.

2
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.480
So how do its seeds get to new places?

3
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.340
An old fuzzy sock pulled over your shoe can catch some in the act.

4
00:00:12.383 --> 00:00:17.523
In about thirty-five minutes, your child practices looking closely, sorting by a

5
00:00:17.523 --> 00:00:18.903
rule, and testing a guess.

6
00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:25.223
They notice how seed shapes help seeds travel by wind, water, and animals, including

7
00:00:25.223 --> 00:00:26.223
people.

8
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:29.127
Ask, 'How do seeds get to new places?'

9
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:30.767
Write down their ideas.

10
00:00:32.227 --> 00:00:33.467
Slip the sock over one shoe.

11
00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:37.667
Walk slowly along the weedy edge for three to five minutes.

12
00:00:39.127 --> 00:00:42.367
Collect only seeds you know are safe to handle, like maple keys.

13
00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:45.437
Stay outdoors to check the sock.

14
00:00:46.677 --> 00:00:47.797
Lay it on white paper.

15
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:52.537
You pull off what stuck with tweezers, looking for ticks as you go.

16
00:00:53.777 --> 00:00:55.717
Then seal the sock in a zip-top bag.

17
00:00:56.717 --> 00:01:00.477
Use a hand lens to see how hooks and spines caught on the sock.

18
00:01:01.837 --> 00:01:05.017
Then make four piles: flies, floats, sticks, gets eaten.

19
00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:08.057
Ask, 'Why does this one go in that pile?'

20
00:01:08.817 --> 00:01:11.177
Some seeds fit two piles, and that is fine.

21
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.950
Stay with your child at a shallow bowl of water.

22
00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:17.530
Ask which seeds will float.

23
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.730
Drop one of each kind into the water.

24
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:23.570
Check again after ten minutes.

25
00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.130
Empty the bowl when you're done.

26
00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:28.903
Now a question for you.

27
00:01:30.043 --> 00:01:32.783
Stand on the floor and hold a whole maple key up high.

28
00:01:34.343 --> 00:01:35.663
Snap the wing off another key.

29
00:01:36.883 --> 00:01:38.263
Hold both at the same height.

30
00:01:39.483 --> 00:01:41.183
Which do you predict will land first?

31
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:49.687
The key without its wing hits the floor much sooner.

32
00:01:50.847 --> 00:01:54.847
The whole key spins as it falls, and the spinning wing slows its drop.

33
00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:58.507
A breeze then has more time to push it sideways.

34
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:01.327
Time three drops of each.

35
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.330
Choose a patch where picking is allowed.

36
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.170
Stay within arm's reach outdoors.

37
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.650
Never taste any part of a wild plant.

38
00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.090
Keep acorns, small seeds, and burrs away from children under four.

39
00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.890
Call nine one one if choking.

40
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:24.430
If anyone swallows berries or seeds from the activity, call Poison Help.

41
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.100
Ticks live in tall grass.

42
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.180
Wear closed shoes and tuck long pants into socks.

43
00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.020
Before going inside, check your child's skin, scalp, hair, and clothes for ticks.

44
00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.640
Wash hands with soap after handling seeds.

45
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.740
Look back at your child's first ideas.

46
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.300
Ask, 'How did the seeds on the sock get there?'

47
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.700
Any seeds that stuck show how people can carry them.

48
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.300
If the sock stayed empty, try again later.
